WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.640
- You're gonna learn how
to communicate like a CEO

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.110
or other top leader.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.360
This is part three in a three-part series

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.070
on How to Become a Leader.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:09.600
And we've now worked our way up

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.220
to five ways CEOs communicate
differently than frontline

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.870
or even mid-level leaders.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.460
The big overarching skill

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.320
that separates top leaders from the rest

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.500
is what leadership researcher
Robert Katz describes

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.190
as the ability to think and
talk about the big picture.

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:29.490
Compared to frontline
supervisors or mid-level leaders,

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.380
leaders at the highest levels must think

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.260
and speak about the entire organization.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.160
I'm talking about CEOs, presidents,
the rest of the C-suite.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.510
But even if you're the founder

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.210
or owner of a small
business with just a handful

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:45.180
of employees, you must
do these five skills.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.320
Now, as you'll see,

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.130
these five big picture communication
skills are more complex

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.920
and demanding than most
other communication skills.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.860
Rest assured, anybody can improve

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.260
with time, guidance, and effort.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.810
So use this video as a self-assessment

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.370
of what skills you're already good at

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.200
and the ones you wanna improve.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.860
The number one big picture
way to communicate like a CEO

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:12.720
is to know and communicate
the deeper meaning of work.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.290
Many leaders and authors
call this the why question.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.480
Even before they start an organization,

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.410
founding leaders are driven by
a clear purpose, a key reason

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.000
for doing what they're doing.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.010
You'll see that spelled
out in their vision

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:29.490
and mission statements and big
goals that they're chasing.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.530
All these help provide the context

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:34.020
and the explanation to help
everybody on the team understand

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.240
the deeper meaning of the work.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.290
Now, it's true that some of
those website vision statements

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:43.530
and mission statements sound
like marketing points really,

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.780
but I'm talking about
the vision statements

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.370
that really hit home.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.740
For example, what's the deeper meaning

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.390
that drives the Khan Academy

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:54.210
to keep cranking out educational videos?

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:57.390
The founder, Sal Khan, he has a why

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.340
and that is to provide a free

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.750
and world class education
to anyone anywhere.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.000
It's his reason for being.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.050
Those employees are not simply
recording and editing videos.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.540
The deeper meaning they all have in mind

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.780
is that they wanna educate the
world, and that's powerful.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.480
It's the CEO's job to communicate that.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.560
For our self-assessment,

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.410
on a scale of one to 10,
how good are you at seeing

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:25.710
and articulating the big
picture deeper meaning of work?

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.680
Top leaders must be
the number one advocate

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.680
to communicate the deeper meaning of work.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.290
The number two way that CEOs communicate

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:35.550
is that they must inspire

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.817
and motivate others to
achieve great things together.

00:02:38.817 --> 00:02:41.850
And we all need individual
inspiration and motivation.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:45.180
That's true, but the CEO or
the President must get us

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.730
to work as one big team together

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.230
and show us that we're all part

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.240
of something bigger than ourselves.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.450
That's why when JFK gave
his moonshot speech,

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:57.720
he was explicit about who
would be accomplishing this.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.140
He said, "I believe this
nation should commit itself

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.220
to achieving the goal

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.150
before this decade is out
of landing a man on the moon

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:09.150
and returning him safely to the Earth."

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:12.870
He didn't say, for example,
in my presidency or NASA

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:16.320
or astronauts, he said, "This nation."

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.190
He's saying, we, together,
we are going to do this.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:21.390
Now we create organizations

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:24.690
because individuals can't
do these things alone.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.750
And when Neil Armstrong put
his feet on the moon finally

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:30.600
for the first time before
the end of the decade,

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.270
he was speaking from that same mindset.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.940
He didn't even say, "This
is a victory for America."

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.390
He said, "It was a
giant leap for mankind."

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:41.760
He was saying, we did this.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.570
And in response, literally,
the entire planet celebrated.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.730
In more everyday, practical ways,

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:50.280
I've heard numerous top leaders inspire

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.470
and motivate others to
achieve great things together

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:54.450
by telling success stories

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:56.850
or stories about humble beginnings

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:58.950
and how far we've come together.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.630
I've been working with the president

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:01.860
of a billion dollar company

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:05.820
and he tells stories all
the time about how we,

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:08.730
the company, grew from
less than 25 employees

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:10.680
to over 1,000 employees,

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.510
and that each of those employees is part

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:14.820
of that success and legacy.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.440
He doesn't inspire and motivate us

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.360
by talking about how he did it.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:21.930
He talks genuinely
about how we all did it.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.490
Question for you from one to 10,

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:24.990
how good are you at inspiring

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:27.300
and motivating people to work as a team

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.820
to achieve great things together?

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:33.150
Now, it's a bonus if you're in
the mid-level management area

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:34.710
or frontline supervisor area,

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:38.880
and you already talk like this,
but top leaders must do it.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.490
Number three is that the CEO must know

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.350
and talk about how the various parts

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.870
of the organization work together.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.000
Individual contributors in contrast,

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.950
talk mostly about their own expertise,

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:52.770
department and division
leaders talk about their shops,

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:55.050
but the top leaders must talk about

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:57.960
how the entire enterprise works together.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.940
Now, maybe that leader
rose through the ranks

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.210
and came up through IT
or operations or finance,

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:06.990
but once they're the top
leader, the CEO, the president,

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:09.420
they're now leading all of those areas

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:10.470
and they must talk in ways

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:12.870
that help everybody see the organization

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.300
and those different parts
working, coordinating,

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:16.830
and cooperating together.

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.680
As organizations grow,
they quickly separate

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.420
into what we call silos.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:23.760
On a farm, you keep the corn in one silo

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.970
and the wheat in another
silo so they don't mix.

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:28.440
And that's what happens in organizations.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:29.910
They silo themselves

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:33.540
and stop sharing information,
resources, talent.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:34.590
But top leaders know

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:37.170
that organizations work
best when they are running

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:40.080
like interconnected,
interdependent systems

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.840
where the subparts talk
to each other more openly

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:45.090
and work together more freely.

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:48.223
Question for you, how skilled are you,

00:05:48.223 --> 00:05:49.056
from one to 10, at understanding

00:05:49.056 --> 00:05:51.720
and talking about how
all of the various parts

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:54.120
of the organization work together?

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.970
The top leaders are really
the only individuals position

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:58.440
for this responsibility,

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:01.110
and that's why they must
be the ones to talk about

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:02.850
how all the parts work together.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:05.250
Now, I've seen very few popular videos

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:06.690
and mainstream articles written

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:10.110
about the next two CEO
skills, but they're often

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:13.620
what help organizations
succeed in the long run.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:16.350
Number four is that the CEO must engage

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:19.470
in environmental scanning and sense-making

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:21.300
for their organization.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:22.200
The first half of this

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.180
is what we call environmental scanning.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:26.850
That's when the top
leaders deliberately look

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:29.820
at external variables that
can influence the organization

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:33.240
like market trends,
government policy changes,

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:37.170
competitors' activities,
innovations and technology changes,

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:40.110
demographic changes, many
other external variables.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.390
They must also track internal variables

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:46.110
like workplace morale and
satisfaction, employee turnover,

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:48.450
and other measures of success.

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:50.820
Top leaders must scan for changes

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:53.760
and then help the organization
adapt to those changes

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.950
so they're not caught on their back foot

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:57.900
falling behind the industry.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:00.930
The second half of environmental
scanning is making sense

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:04.530
of those or interpreting
these factors for employees,

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:07.380
and this should not be
spin or public relations.

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:09.540
The CEO's perspective should be an honest

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:12.840
and helpful sense-making
process of what's happening

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.480
around the organization
and in the organization.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:16.830
Are tariffs going up?

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:18.120
How's that going to affect us?

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.430
Another pizza place
opened across the street.

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:22.590
What does that mean for ours?

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:25.530
Three of our top people
left, what do we do now?

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:29.070
From one to 10, how would you
rate yourself in your ability

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:31.860
to scan the environment
and interpret those changes

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:33.960
and how they'll affect your team?

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:35.520
Followers want to hear

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.250
what the top leaders
think about those changes,

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:39.630
what they see coming,

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:42.150
and what top leaders plan to do about it.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:44.400
Number five, CEOs must
communicate effectively

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.680
with external stakeholders.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:49.410
Organizations are not isolated entities.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:52.290
They are connected to other organizations.

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:55.290
CEOs are positioned at
least at first to work best

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:58.260
with suppliers, vendors, local, federal

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:02.220
and government agencies,
potential partner organizations.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.470
When you see travel websites, for example.

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:07.440
Airlines, hotels, car rental
agencies, all working together,

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:09.060
that's not an accident.

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:11.910
That's the result of top
leaders doing their best

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:14.220
to create collaborative relationships.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:17.520
Those relationships should
be based on mutual benefits

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.080
and a fair exchange of value

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:21.390
between those external stakeholders.

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:23.070
Small organizations do this too,

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:24.810
but in more straightforward ways.

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:27.900
I have a small business that's
wrapped around this channel

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:31.050
where I work with website
people, video, editors,

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:34.530
social media managers,
sponsors, an accounting firm.

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:37.020
I do workshops for client organizations

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:39.120
and coach top corporate leaders.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.030
It's part of my job to
make sure we're all getting

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:44.820
what we want out of these relationships.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.280
From one to 10, how skilled
are you at collaborating

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:49.320
with external stakeholders?

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:50.940
Working with external stakeholders

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:52.595
should elevate everybody's business

00:08:52.595 --> 00:08:55.980
and even help you weather
the storms when businesses

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:58.560
are in trouble or things
are not going too well.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.500
That's why it's a necessary
part of the CEO's job

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:04.860
to manage these relationships
as effectively as possible.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:06.870
Here are the top five communication skills

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:09.120
and responsibilities of CEOs.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:11.100
Based upon your self-assessment scores,

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:12.330
which of these would you consider

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.400
to be the most natural for you

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:16.800
and which of them do you
need the most work on

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:18.780
to gain experience as a leader?

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:20.910
They're all learnable.

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:22.530
Now, since this is part
three of the series,

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:24.960
if you've watched all three
parts, please say hello

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:27.210
and tell me you've
completed the entire series

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:28.800
in a comment below.

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:29.940
And I'll congratulate you

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:32.520
and give you a heart to your comment.

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:34.890
Again, feel free to visit
the free page on my website.

00:09:34.890 --> 00:09:36.570
It's a great place to learn

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:37.920
about communication and leadership.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:39.270
You can start by that class

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:41.730
with the top five essential
communication skills

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:43.800
that all professionals should have.

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:44.730
Until next time, thanks.

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:46.430
God bless and I will see you soon.

